issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**
I have integrated a DPS310 sensor from Infineon to nRF9160 via I2C and trying to get the example DSP310 sample application provided in the samples to work. However, after checking and rechecking all the configurations, I keep getting

`Could not get DPS310 device`

which simply tells me that the application is failing to get the device binding.

```
const struct device *dev = device_get_binding(DT_LABEL(DT_INST(0, infineon_dps310)));

if (dev == NULL) {
	printk("Could not get DPS310 device\n");
	return;
}
```

To diagnose this issue, I have enabled the logs at the I2C and the DPS310 driver level as follows
```
CONFIG_LOG=y
CONFIG_SENSOR_LOG_LEVEL_DBG=y
CONFIG_I2C_LOG_LEVEL_DBG=y
```

and found the following:

```
/* Initialize the sensor and apply the configuration */
static int dps310_init(const struct device *dev)
{
	struct dps310_data *data = dev->data;
	const struct dps310_cfg *config = dev->config;

	data->i2c_master = device_get_binding(config->i2c_bus_name);
	if (data->i2c_master == NULL) {
		LOG_ERR("Failed to get I2C device");
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	uint8_t product_id = 0;
	int res = i2c_reg_read_byte(data->i2c_master, config->i2c_addr,
				 IFX_DPS310_REG_ADDR_PRODUCT_ID, &product_id);

	if (res < 0) {
		LOG_ERR("No device found");
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	if (product_id != IFX_DPS310_PRODUCT_ID) {
		LOG_ERR("Device is not a DPS310");
		return -EINVAL;
	}
	else
	{
		LOG_DBG("DPS310 product_id: 0x%02x", product_id);
	}
	
	LOG_DBG("Init DPS310");
	/* give the sensor time to load the calibration data */
	k_sleep(K_MSEC(40));

	/* wait for the sensor to load the calibration data */
	if (!poll_rdy(data, config, REG_ADDR_MEAS_CFG,
		      IFX_DPS310_REG_ADDR_MEAS_CFG_SELF_INIT_OK)) {
		LOG_DBG("Sensor not ready");
		return -EIO;
	}

	/* read calibration coefficients */
	uint8_t raw_coef[18] = { 0 };

	res = i2c_write_read(data->i2c_master, config->i2c_addr,
			     &REG_ADDR_CALIB_COEFF_0, 1, &raw_coef, 18);
	if (res < 0) {
		LOG_WRN("I2C error: %d", res);
		return -EIO;
	}

	/* convert calibration coefficients */
	dps310_calib_coeff_creation(raw_coef, &data->comp);

	/*
	 * check which temperature sensor was used for calibration and use it
	 * for measurements.
	 */
	uint8_t tmp_coef_srce = 0;

	res = i2c_write_read(data->i2c_master, config->i2c_addr,
			     &REG_ADDR_COEF_SRCE, 1, &tmp_coef_srce, 18);
	if (res < 0) {
		LOG_WRN("I2C error: %d", res);
		return -EIO;
	}

	/* clear all other bits */
	tmp_coef_srce &= IFX_DPS310_COEF_SRCE_MASK;

	/* merge with temperature measurement configuration */
	tmp_coef_srce |= IFX_DPS310_TMP_CFG;

	/* set complete configuration in one write */
	const uint8_t config_seq[] = {
		IFX_DPS310_REG_ADDR_PRS_CFG,	/* start register address */
		IFX_DPS310_PSR_CFG,		/* PSR_CFG */
		tmp_coef_srce,			/* TMP_CFG */
		0x00,				/* MEAS_CFG */
		DPS310_CFG_REG			/* CFG_REG */
	};

	res = i2c_write(data->i2c_master, config_seq, sizeof(config_seq),
			config->i2c_addr);
	if (res < 0) {
		LOG_WRN("I2C error: %d", res);
		return -EIO;
	}

	dps310_hw_bug_fix(data, config);
	dps310_measure_tmp_psr(data, config);

	LOG_DBG("Init OK");
	return 0;
}
```
In the DPS310 driver `dps310_init()` function;
The code runs as expected up to reading the product ID and then the  `i2c_write_read()` call for reading calibration coefficients fails and gives a negative value for input/output error failing to initialize the driver properly.
```
I2C error: -5 
```
What is surprising that the I2C call for reading the product ID from the sensor register works and it gives the correct product id as `0x10h` which indicates that connections and i2c address are correct.

Looking deeper into the code, the ` i2c_reg_read_byte()` actually returns ` i2c_write_read()`. However, directly calling the ` i2c_write_read()` in the DPS310 driver layer is not working as expected. So why does one I2C API call works while the other does not? 

```
static inline int i2c_reg_read_byte(const struct device *dev,
				    uint16_t dev_addr,
				    uint8_t reg_addr, uint8_t *value)
{
	return i2c_write_read(dev, dev_addr,
			      &reg_addr, sizeof(reg_addr),
			      value, sizeof(*value));
}
```

**To Reproduce**
Add the following config to the overlay file:

```
/*
 * A DPS310 attached to the I2C pins: SDA:26, SCL:27
 */

&i2c1 {
	compatible = "nordic,nrf-twim";
	sda-pin = < 0x1a >;
	scl-pin = < 0x1b >;	
	status = "okay";
	DPS310@77 {
		status = "okay";
		compatible = "infineon,dps310";
		reg = <0x77>;
		label = "DPS310";
	};
};

&uart1 {
	status = "disabled";
};
```
and the following to the prj.conf file

```
CONFIG_I2C=y
CONFIG_SENSOR=y
CONFIG_DPS310=y
CONFIG_LOG=y
CONFIG_SENSOR_LOG_LEVEL_DBG=y
CONFIG_I2C_LOG_LEVEL_DBG=y
```

Then build and flash the application as follows:

```
cd samples/sensor/dps310
west build -b nrf9160dk_nrf9160 --pristine
west flash
```

**Expected behavior**
On application startup, log message says:

```
Could not get DPS310 device
```

**Impact**
Simply annoying and unreliable as now I am unsure that which of these sensor drivers might be working or broken unless each one is retested. Also, one might have to rewrite the specific sensor driver to make it work for their specific platform which is not what one might expect. 

**Environment:**
 - MacOS
 - nRF Connect SDK + Zephyr
 - v1.5.1

**Additional context**
After reading other threads, this might the ongoing and lingering i2c issue in Zephyr as mentioned [here](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues/33440) and [here](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues/27675).
